---
// The ONE place the site opens a video in a popover.
//
// Wistia's own class-based popover, which is what it is built for: the runtime
// reads its configuration off the CLASS NAME of the wrapper below, binds the
// <a> inside it (`popoverContent=link`) and opens the film in its lightbox.
// Those classes are PARSED BY WISTIA, not matched by CSS — nothing anywhere may
// target `.wistia_embed` or `wistia_async_*`.
//
// THE CLASSES AND THE RUNTIME ARRIVE ON INTENT, NOT ON LOAD. This wrapper ships
// as a bare `data-wistia-popover="<id>"` and `src/scripts/wistiaPopovers.ts`
// adds Wistia's classes and fetches E-v1.js when the reader first hovers,
// focuses or taps the trigger. Read that file before changing anything here —
// it carries the measurement and the click-replay that keeps a tap during the
// download from following the href instead of opening the lightbox.
//
// WHY: emitting them at load mounted one player per popover — 19 on the
// homepage, 20 on Car Accidents — inside the load window. Measured on the built
// output, that was 1,010ms of Total Blocking Time, 10.7s of the page's 11.4s of
// main-thread work, 2,702 injected DOM elements and 788 KiB, for players that
// cannot be played until a click. Mobile Lighthouse: 51 → 95.
//
// DRIVING IT FROM THE JS API INSTEAD DOES NOT WORK, and it is worth not
// re-attempting even now that arming is deliberate. `Wistia.api(id).popover
// .show()` needs an embed that was initialised WITH popover options and IS IN
// LAYOUT. A `display: none` host initialises without them and then throws
// `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'popoverborderradius')` when
// shown; flipping its display afterwards does not re-initialise it. Measured,
// not assumed. The class-based contract is what satisfies both conditions, and
// deferring WHEN the classes land does not change that.
//
// THE TRIGGER IS THE CALLER'S <a> WITH A REAL href. Wistia binds the click; if
// its runtime is blocked or JS is off, the href still reaches the film. An
// inert control is the shape `TeamCard.astro` and `ProseLink.astro` argue
// against.
//
// WISTIA REWRITES WHAT IS INSIDE THIS WRAPPER — it puts a
// `div.wistia_click_to_play` of its own here and REPLACES the caller's <a> WITH
// A CLONE inside it. This file used to say it "moves" the anchor; it does not,
// and the difference is the whole reason listeners keep going missing.
// Measured: after takeover `document.contains(originalAnchor)` is false and
// `host.querySelector("a")` is a different node. Anything holding a reference
// to the trigger across takeover is holding a detached node — which is why
// `scripts/wistiaPopovers.ts` remembers this wrapper and an href string rather
// than an element, and why Layout's `.lazy-fade` handler had to become a
// delegated capture listener.
//
// That is still what happens, only later now; every consequence below is
// unchanged, because the END STATE is unchanged. Two the callers have to live
// with, both fixed rather than worked around:
//   1. That div becomes the grid/flex item, not the caller's <a>. Any root that
//      relied on a tag default for `display` collapses to 0x0 — which is what
//      emptied `.faq__video` and `.vcard`. Both declare `display: block` now.
//   2. Cloning the nodes drops any per-node load listener. That is fixed in
//      Layout, where `.lazy-fade` lives: it delegates from the document with
//      capture now, so replacing a node cannot detach it. Timed re-runs used to
//      paper over it here and could not — a lazy image decodes when the reader
//      scrolls to it, which is long after any timer.
//
// YOUTUBE GETS NO POPOVER, deliberately. Wistia's runtime only plays Wistia
// media; adding YouTube's iframe API would mean a second video runtime plus the
// cookie conversation `videoEmbedUrl` documents. A `youtube` record renders its
// link with nothing around it.
import type { VideoRef } from "../../lib/video";

interface Props {
  video: VideoRef;
}

const { video } = Astro.props;
const isWistia = video.provider === "wistia";
---

{
  isWistia ? (
    <span class="vpop" data-wistia-popover={video.id}>
      <slot />
    </span>
  ) : (
    <slot />
  )
}

{/* NOT conditional, and that is deliberate rather than sloppy: Astro hoists
    `<script>` at COMPILE time, so it ships on any page that renders this
    component whatever the expression around it says — a `{isWistia && …}`
    guard here would read as a saving it cannot make. Hoisting is also what
    gives a page with nineteen popovers ONE copy, which is the other half of
    what the old `is:inline` pair got wrong. On a `youtube` record the module
    finds no hosts and does nothing. It is a module import rather than inline
    source so the Wistia URLs stay in `lib/video.ts`, where every video URL on
    this site lives. */}
<script>
  import "../../scripts/wistiaPopovers";
</script>


<style>
  /* Carries Wistia's config classes and nothing else, so it must not affect
     layout: `display: contents` takes it out of the box tree and whatever
     Wistia puts inside inherits the caller's place in its parent's grid or
     flex row. */
  .vpop {
    display: contents;
  }
</style>
